  Postgres for storage. No SPA framework — the existing [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is a plain page, the UI is a list and a form, and a
  client-side framework would be more build tooling than the feature justifies. If the UI later grows to need one, the
  server keeps its JSON endpoints and nothing here is wasted.

  [1mLayers, each independently testable:[0m

  - [38;5;153mdb/[39m — connection, migrations, no business logic.
  - [38;5;153mdomain/tasks.ts[39m — the single write path. Every mutation is a function here ([38;5;153mcreateTask[39m, [38;5;153mupdateTask[39m, [38;5;153massignTask[39m, …)
    that takes an actor and a change, and writes the task row plus its [38;5;153mtask_events[39m rows in one transaction. [1mThis is the
[0m    [1mchokepoint the outbox decision depends on[0m; handlers never write to [38;5;153mtasks[39m directly.
  - [38;5;153mdomain/auth.ts[39m — magic-link issuance, token verification, session creation.
  - [38;5;153mroutes/[39m — HTTP only: parse, authorize, call a domain function, render or serialize.
  - [38;5;153mviews/[39m — HTML templates.
  - [38;5;153mmail/[39m — a [38;5;153mMailer[39m interface with a console implementation for dev and one real transport. Sub-project 3 reuses this
    rather than inventing its own sender.

  [1mData flow for a change:[0m request → route (authenticate, validate) → domain function → transaction { update [38;5;153mtasks[39m;
  insert [38;5;153mtask_events[39m } → commit → re-render. Notifications will later consume [38;5;153mtask_events[39m, and nothing above that line
  has to change.

  [1mDeliberately excluded (YAGNI):[0m no job queue, no websockets, no real-time push, no notification code of any kind in
  this sub-project. The due-date field is stored and displayed; the [3mscheduler[0m that acts on it belongs to sub-project 3.

  [1mTooling.[0m Nothing is configured yet, and this is the cheapest moment it will ever be to set it up — before there's code
  to retrofit.
